ls (List Directory) - list contents of a directory

Options
-a → show all files (including hidden)
-l → detailed list
-h → human-readable format
eg. ls -la or -ls -l -a (shows detailed list of all folders including hidden and not hidden)
-i - ignores upper or lowercase

rm - delete file
rm -i - ask before deleting
eg. rm -i file2.txt

-r - recursive (is an operation that performs a task on a directory and all it's content including subdirectories and files within)
-f -  force delete
eg. rm -rf test/

mkdir (create directory)
-p - parent directory
e.g mkdir -p Linux/Ubuntu/test (creates Linux dir, Ubuntu folder & test file)

rmdir (removes only empty directory)
To remove parent directory:
rmdir -p Linux/Ubuntu(only if they are empty)

file (check file type)
eg. file file1.txt

touch (create file)

man (Manual) - get documentation for any command
eg. man ls

pwd (Print Working Directory) - shows your current location in the system

cd (Change Directory)
Used to move between directories
cd .. - go one step back

COPY/MOVE
cp (Copy)
cp file1.txt filecopy.txt (copies content in file1 to filecopy)

To copy to another directory:
cp file1.txt /home/user/documents/
Copy directories:
cp -r folder1 folder2

mv (move/rename)
eg. mv oldname.txt newname.txt
Move + rename:
mv file2.txt test/file3.txt (moves file2 into test/ directory and renames it to file3)

